1. Permitted Use

   You may use Kumi for any purpose, including commercial purposes,
   within Your organization, without obtaining a Commercial License.
   The following activities are expressly permitted.

   1(a). Internal Use

   You may:

   - install and operate any number of Kumi instances;
   - create any number of organizations, workspaces, and projects;
   - use Kumi for internal business operations;
   - modify, extend, and customize Kumi for Your organization's own
     use;
   - use Kumi in production environments; and
   - use Kumi as part of Your organization's internal commercial
     activities.

   There is no restriction on the number of users, instances, projects,
   or deployments used internally by Your organization.

   1(b). Guest Access and Collaboration

   You may invite Guests into Kumi instances operated by Your
   organization. Guest access is considered part of Your organization's
   permitted internal use, provided that:

   1. Guests use Kumi only to collaborate with Your organization on
      Your organization's own specific projects or activities;
   2. You do not provide Guests with an independent instance,
      organization, or workspace for managing projects or operations
      unrelated to Your organization;
   3. access to Kumi is not itself the product, platform, or service
      that You sell, license, or commercially provide to Guests;
   4. You do not use Guest access to give third parties that primarily
      manage their own workflows a general external user community,
      client portal, or multi-party collaboration platform; and
   5. Guests do not receive, through that access, an independent right
      to operate or commercially exploit Kumi.

   For clarity, inviting a client, supplier, business partner, or
   external collaborator to participate in Your organization's projects
   is permitted. Providing third parties with independent Kumi
   environments for their own unrelated business operations is not
   permitted without a Commercial License.

   1(c). Installation, Consulting, and Technical Services

   You may install, configure, deploy, maintain, support, and customize
   Kumi for another organization, whether for a fee or without charge.

   The organization receiving these services must:

   1. operate and own its own dedicated Kumi instance on infrastructure
      controlled by that receiving organization, for its own
      organizational purposes;
   2. exercise direct administrative control over its own users,
      organizations, workspaces, and projects; and
   3. be directly responsible for complying with this License.

   In such circumstances, the receiving organization is the one that
   operates the instance, for its own use, and this License applies to
   it directly. It needs no Commercial License for that use.

   A service provider may provide infrastructure setup, technical
   support, maintenance, upgrades, configuration, and development
   services under the direction and control of that organization.

   However, hosting Kumi on the service provider's own infrastructure,
   operating or managing a Kumi instance on behalf of another
   organization, administering workspaces for clients, or charging
   recurring hosting, subscription, seat-based, or usage-based fees
   for ongoing operation of Kumi falls under Condition 2 and requires a
   Commercial License.

   For clarity, charging one-time or recurring fees for the
   maintenance, support, upgrade, or development of an instance that
   the receiving organization itself operates on infrastructure it
   controls is permitted, and is not a fee for the ongoing operation
   of Kumi.

   1(d). Publication and Distribution of Source Code

   You may publish, share, and distribute the original or modified
   source code of Kumi in public or private repositories. Such
   publication and distribution must comply with every condition of
   this License.

   Making source code publicly available does not grant recipients any
   permission to engage in activities restricted under Condition 2 or
   Condition 3.

   Any Derivative Work or modified version of Kumi distributed by You
   must remain subject to the restrictions of Part I, and You may not
   impose additional or different license terms that purport to waive,
   override, or weaken Part I.

   All redistributions must comply with Condition 4(a) and
   Condition 7(d).

2. Hosted Services and Commercial Distribution

   Unless expressly authorized by a Commercial License, You may not
   engage in any of the following activities.

   2(a). Hosted or Managed Services

   You may not operate, provide, or offer Kumi to persons or
   organizations outside Your organization as a hosted, managed, or
   remotely operated service.

   This restriction applies where third parties receive access to or
   use of all or a substantial part of Kumi's functionality through an
   instance operated or controlled by You. Examples include, without
   limitation:

   - offering Kumi as a public or private SaaS platform;
   - providing third parties with Kumi accounts, organizations, or
     workspaces for their independent use;
   - operating Kumi instances for other organizations as a managed
     service;
   - providing Kumi as a subscription-based or usage-based service;
   - offering free or paid access to Kumi as an independently usable
     service; and
   - operating a shared or multi-tenant Kumi environment for unrelated
     third parties.

   This restriction applies regardless of whether You:

   - charge a subscription, seat, or usage fee;
   - generate advertising, platform, commission, referral, or other
     direct or indirect revenue;
   - bundle access to Kumi with consulting, agency, or other paid
     services;
   - provide the service free of charge;
   - offer the service to the public or only to selected organizations;
     or
   - operate the service directly or through an affiliated entity.

   For clarity, a third party's use of Kumi for its own independent
   projects or business operations is not considered Your
   organization's internal use merely because You provide access
   through an instance that You operate.

   2(b). Embedding in Commercial Products or Services

   You may not include, distribute, sublicense, or otherwise
   incorporate Kumi, in whole or in Substantial Part, as a component of
   a product or service that is sold, licensed, subscribed to, or
   otherwise commercially distributed or provided to third parties.

   This restriction applies whether Kumi is distributed or incorporated:

   - as source code;
   - as compiled or executable software;
   - as a container image;
   - as a bundled application;
   - as a backend engine, worker, or microservice component;
   - as a separately packaged module or library;
   - with its user interface removed or replaced; or
   - under a different name or product identity.

   Changing Kumi's name, modifying its source code, splitting it into
   separate components, replacing its frontend, or distributing those
   components through different repositories does not exempt the
   resulting work from this Condition.

   2(c). API Integration and Interoperability

   Merely communicating with Kumi through documented APIs, protocols,
   or integration interfaces does not, by itself, constitute embedding
   Kumi into a commercial product or service.

   You may independently develop commercial software that interoperates
   with Kumi, provided that:

   1. Your software does not redistribute or incorporate Kumi or a
      Substantial Part of Kumi;
   2. Your software does not operate or provide access to Kumi as a
      hosted or managed service in violation of Condition 2(a);
   3. Your software does not act as a proxy, wrapper, API gateway, or
      pass-through layer whose primary commercial value or core
      functionality substantially relies on Kumi's execution, data
      processing, or workflow capabilities, provided to third parties
      through a Kumi instance that You operate or control; and
   4. Your integration does not otherwise circumvent the restrictions in
      this License.

   For clarity, item 3 does not cover software that connects to a Kumi
   instance operated by the software's own user or by that user's
   organization.

   This exception does not grant permission to host Kumi for third
   parties or to commercially distribute Kumi functionality without the
   required Commercial License.

   2(d). Separate Commercial Authorization

   Any activity restricted under this Condition requires a Commercial
   License expressly granted by the Licensor.

   Each person or organization that engages in an activity restricted
   under this Condition, including each Operator, must obtain its own
   Commercial License. A Commercial License granted to one person or
   organization does not automatically extend to:

   - its customers;
   - its affiliates;
   - its service providers;
   - its contractors;
   - other Operators; or
   - recipients of Kumi distributed by that person or organization.

   A Commercial License is valid strictly within the scope expressly
   specified in the written permission granted by the Licensor
   (including, without limitation, authorized products, domains,
   deployments, tenant or user limits, revenue thresholds, and
   duration). Any use exceeding the expressly authorized scope
   constitutes an unlicensed use in breach of this License.

3. Branding and Attribution in the Kumi Interface

   Unless the Licensor has expressly granted a Branding Waiver in
   writing, You may not remove, replace, hide, obscure, or materially
   alter:

   1. the Kumi name;
   2. the Kumi logo;
   3. copyright notices;
   4. author attribution; or
   5. other Kumi branding or attribution information displayed by a
      Kumi Interface.

   This restriction applies to original and modified versions of a
   Kumi Interface.

   You may modify the layout, styling, colors, and other visual
   elements of a Kumi Interface, provided that the Kumi name, logo, and
   required attribution information remain intact, legible, and
   prominently visible in their designated or equivalent interface
   locations without requiring users to navigate to hidden or obscure
   menus.

   A Branding Waiver does not grant permission to engage in any
   activity restricted under Condition 2.

   This Condition does not apply where no Kumi Interface is
   redistributed or operated, including circumstances where You use
   only:

   - the Kumi server API;
   - the `kumi` command-line tool;
   - the Kumi MCP server; or
   - other non-interface components of Kumi.

   Condition 4 applies to those circumstances.

4. Attribution When No Kumi Interface Is Used

   If You redistribute Kumi, or operate a product or service built on
   Kumi, without redistributing or operating a Kumi Interface, You
   must do both of the following.

   4(a). Preserve Notices

   Retain all applicable copyright, patent, trademark, and attribution
   notices included in the original source code and any NOTICE file.
   You must also comply with the notice preservation requirements in
   Part II, Section 4.

   4(b). Provide Attribution to Users

   Include a clear statement, in documentation or other legal or
   informational materials made available to Your users, that the
   product or service is built on Kumi. The statement must include a
   link to https://github.com/OmarHung/kumi. For example:

      "This product uses Kumi, a source-available software project
      licensed under the Kumi License."

   The attribution must be reasonably accessible to users, such as in:

   - product documentation;
   - an About page;
   - a Legal Notices page;
   - a Third-Party Licenses page; or
   - where no user-facing interface exists (such as backend services,
     CLI tools, or MCP servers), in the repository README, deployment
     documentation, release notes, or accompanying legal/notice files.

   You may not imply that the Licensor endorses, sponsors, or is
   affiliated with Your product or service without express written
   permission.

